WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  to me black history month is a time for  reflection and inspiration  reflecting on the intellectual and  societal contributions the black  community has made the world  reflecting on the visionaries who  rallied against seemingly insurmountable  prejudice and violence to make those  contributions  and reflecting on those lost along the  way  this month reminds me that the trials of  our present will one day become the  reflections of our tomorrow and inspires  me to seek to be counted among those who  have contributed to that future  [Music]  black history month is an occasion to  reflect on the struggle of the past  generations for freedom  give thanks to our ancestors for being  brave and appreciate the inspiring  progress people from the african  diaspora have made  without them  walls and barriers would not have been  broken down  looking forward we can learn from this  as we seek to make our future a better  and more inclusive one for those who  come after us  black history month is a time for me to  reflect and celebrate  i reflect on the words in spite of when  i think about black history month i  often think about the magnitude of what  black people across generations have  accomplished almost always in spite of  something whether it be adversity or  presumption of inequality  black history month is also a time when  i celebrate black people's prevailing  resilience  and the vitality that sustains our  influential progress creativity and hope  our world isn't perfect by any means but  black history month provides a healthy  blend of celebration and reflective  criticism  they may sound contradictory but i found  those two to be a necessary pair for  progress driven by hope  black history month is a time to  celebrate all the progress we've made  while acknowledging the immense work  that still needs to be done to get to an  equitable plan  it reminds us that progress is not  linear  but arcs towards justice with enough  work and sacrifice  it's a month of jubilation for the  progress we've made  sorrow for the leaders we've lost  and hope that we can and will leave the  world a better place for future  generations  [Music]  black history month has always been a  complex time for me  a time for learning and celebration to  build a deeper appreciation for the rich  history and progress of the  african-american community  a time for reflection to ask myself what  more can i be doing for the black  community and to also constructively  challenge those in positions of power  around me to do more as well  and lastly a time for fellowship to  navigate all the complexities of the  month with a community that is  incredibly supportive and one that i  could not be more proud to be a part of  black history month is a time to remind  myself of the sacrifices that were made  by my ancestors and the many more that  have come before me  it is a time to reflect on the reasons  why i have the privileges i do today  and the responsibilities that are  attached to them  there's a time to celebrate the  accomplishments of the american negro  ethnicity in spite of the adversity and  institutional challenges that have and  continue to work against it  for me black history month represents a  time of reflection about who i really am  and where i come from  as someone born in africa  raised in canada and now living in the  us i have seen and experienced just how  varied black identity can be  we are far from being a monolith  and yet  we share a relentless spirit that allows  us to not just survive no matter where  we are but more importantly to thrive  so  onwards and upwards we go  [Music]  to me black history month is not only a  time to reflect on the sacrifices  resilience and accomplishments of those  that came before us  it's a time where i recognize and  celebrate the trials and progress we are  making today for our future generations  it's a reminder that we should take time  to reflect  celebrate ourselves and not water  ourselves down  lock history month for me is a  celebration of blackness our successes  our wins our progress our contributions  to the world  our multifaceted and diverse history and  stories  it's also about reflecting on our  progress through adversity while looking  ahead to all the work that still needs  to be done  black history month means making space  to pause reflect  celebrate and connect  it means acknowledging and celebrating  the resilience and beauty of black  people  it means reflecting on black history and  thinking about what i can do to shape  black futures for the better  you

